\section{Joint Transformation Matrices}

The knee transform is planar: every joint frame is a rotation about $z$ by the knee angle $\theta_{k}$ composed with a translation fitted to robot CAD tables of the migrating instantaneous center of rotation. With $\mathbf{R} = \mathbf{R}_{z}(\theta_{k})$, the robot (femur$\to$ICR) transform is $\mathbf{T}_{\text{Pam}} = [\mathbf{R},\; [f_{3}(\theta_{k}),\,f_{4}(\theta_{k}),\,0]^{\top};\; \mathbf{0}^{\top}\;1]$ with $f_{3}, f_{4}$ cubic-spline fits (ICR $x$: 23.30 to 10.47~mm; $y$: $-416.65$ to $-395.66$~mm over $\theta_{k} = 0.17$ to $-2.62$~rad), and the ICR$\to$tibia transform is the pure translation $[f_{13}(\theta_{k}),\,f_{14}(\theta_{k}),\,0]^{\top}$ ($x$: 29.66 to $-7.58$~mm; $y$: 25.97 to 22.33~mm), composed as $\mathbf{T}_{t1\to f} = \mathbf{T}_{t1\to\text{ICR}}\;\mathbf{T}_{\text{Pam}}^{-1}$ over 100 frames spanning $\theta_{k} \in [-120^{\circ}, +10^{\circ}]$ [Provenance: \nolinkurl{buildKneeFlexorContext20mm.m} lines 34--80; \nolinkurl{buildKneeExtContext20mm.m} lines 30--78; \nolinkurl{Functions/ModernRobotics/RpToTrans.m}]. In the archived test sets the per-frame transform maps tibia to femur/ICR and takes the form
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{T}_{k}(\theta_{k}) \;=\;
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \cos\theta_{k} & -\sin\theta_{k} & 0 & d_{x}(\theta_{k})\\
    \sin\theta_{k} & \cos\theta_{k} & 0 & d_{y}(\theta_{k})\\
    0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\
    0 & 0 & 0 & 1
    \end{bmatrix},
    \label{eq:app_Tk}
\end{equation}
with rotation block $\mathbf{R}_{z}(\theta_{k})$ and translation components $d_{x}, d_{y}$ in meters. In the archived flexor test set the translation is constant across the sweep, $[d_{x},\,d_{y},\,0] = [4.5,\,-395.8,\,0]$~mm, and the rotation sweeps from $\mathbf{R}_{z}(-120^{\circ})$ at full flexion to $\mathbf{R}_{z}(+10^{\circ})$ at full extension (\nolinkurl{FlxPinBPASet.mat}, kf(1)).

\section{Bracket Reference Points and Frames}

Table~\ref{tab:app_bracket_points} lists the bracket reference points. Each compliant bracket carries an instantaneous body-fixed frame anchored at its reference point, constructed by two rotations (yaw about $z$, then pitch about the yawed $y$). With $\mathbf{p}_{b}$ the vector from the reference point to the bracket-to-BPA attachment target, the yaw and pitch angles are
\begin{equation}
    \theta_{br} = \operatorname{atan2}(p_{by},\, p_{bx}),
    \qquad
    \phi_{br} = \operatorname{atan2}(p'_{bz},\, p'_{bx})
    \quad\text{with}\quad
    \mathbf{p}'_{b} = \mathbf{R}_{z}(\theta_{br})^{\top}\mathbf{p}_{b},
    \label{eq:app_bracketangles}
\end{equation}
and the bracket-frame pose is
\begin{equation}
    \mathbf{R}_{kbr} = \mathbf{R}_{z}(\theta_{br})\,\mathbf{R}_{y}(\phi_{br})^{\top},
    \qquad
    \mathbf{T}_{kbr} = \left[\mathbf{R}_{kbr},\; \mathbf{p}_{br};\; \mathbf{0}\;\,1\right],
    \label{eq:app_bracketframe}
\end{equation}
so the frame's $x$ axis aims along the nominal load path. The angles are computed per configuration from the bracket geometry, not fixed [Provenance: \nolinkurl{minimizeFlxPin2brk.m} lines 252--298; \nolinkurl{minimizeExtX3.m} lines 461--483]. As executed, however, the flexor evaluator of record assembles \emph{both} of its bracket frames from the yaw rotation alone (the pitch-only, one-transform pose $\mathbf{T}_{kbr} = [\mathbf{R}_{z}(\theta_{br}), \mathbf{p}_{br}; \mathbf{0}\;1]$): insertion bracket at line 289 and origin bracket at line 305 of \nolinkurl{minimizeFlxPin.m}, with the matching pitch-only point reconstructions at lines 318 and 331; the two-rotation assembly of Eq.~\eqref{eq:app_bracketframe} is computed but left commented at lines 288, 304, and 330. For the pinned extensor the two-rotation assembly is live (transMode default \texttt{2trans}; \nolinkurl{minimizeExtX3.m} lines 3--6 and 479--482). Force is transformed into the bracket frame by $\mathbf{RowVecTrans}(\mathbf{T}_{kbr}^{-1}, \mathbf{F}_{k})$ and deformed points are mapped back by $\mathbf{RowVecTrans}(\mathbf{T}_{kbr}, \cdot)$. Because the insertion array $[X_{1},X_{2},X_{1}]$ is $y$-symmetric, the assembly choice is numerically immaterial there; for the origin bracket it is not (see Table~\ref{tab:app_stiffness_arrays}). The pitch-only (yaw-only) variant that the earlier one-transform fit used survives in \nolinkurl{minimizeFlxPinX3.m} lines 200--209.

