\section{Adopted Correction-Term Values}

Table~\ref{tab:app_xi_values} records the correction-term values. The initial single-bracket identification reported with the results figures (Section~\ref{sec:results_torque}) was superseded by the two-stage identification described in Section~\ref{sec:ongoing}: a two-bracket flexor identification over the four tests of Table~\ref{tab:app_flxpin_tests} other than the 47\,cm test, cross-validated with two tests held out per fold (the adopted fold trained on the 48\,cm and 41\,cm tests and validated on the 46\,cm and 40\,cm-tendon tests), followed by an extensor refit that locks $(X_{1}, X_{2})$ to a member of the same flexor Pareto front and solves only the extensor-specific terms $(X_{0}, X_{3})$ on its three-test training set with three tests held out for validation. The pinned-flexor data do not uniquely determine $(X_{1}, X_{2})$ (the cross-validation fronts contain a family of stiffness pairs of comparable fit quality), so the adopted flexor row and the locked extensor refit are two operating points tied to one and the same stiffness pair; Table~\ref{tab:app_xi_values} and the paragraph that follows it state the adopted values and their front-row provenance. The adopted pair was chosen because it is the pair for which both redesign configurations meet the required 5\,\% torque-overshoot target simultaneously: in the adoption sweep the flexor redesign reached a minimum margin of $+5.00$\,\% and the locked extensor redesign $+5.043$\,\% at $-20.2^{\circ}$ (\nolinkurl{Dig_out/frommat_flx_20260920.log} and \nolinkurl{Dig_out/Opt_flxr77_20260920.log}); the refined redesigns improve this to $+5.01$\,\% (flexor) and $+5.02$\,\% (extensor).

