\section{Stiffness Arrays and Compliance}

Every configuration models bracket compliance as a diagonal Hooke-law spring in the bracket frame, $\mathbf{K}_{br} = \operatorname{diag}(k_{1}, k_{2}, k_{3})$ newtons per meter. The scalar compliance along the force path and its series combinations are
\begin{equation}
    c_{b} = \hat{\mathbf{u}}^{\top}\mathbf{K}_{br}^{-1}\hat{\mathbf{u}},
    \qquad
    c_{\text{eff}} = c_{b} + c_{\text{ten}} \;(+\,c_{b2}\ \text{for the second bracket}),
    \label{eq:app_compliance}
\end{equation}
exactly as in Equations~(\ref{eq:bktstiffness})--(\ref{eq:keff}); when an artificial tendon is present its axial spring ($\times2$ cables, effective steel area $1.51\times10^{-6}$~m$^{2}$, $E = 193$~GPa) adds in series, and the force balance of Equation~\eqref{eq:complianceforcebalance} is solved per frame by \texttt{fzero} on the contraction interval [Provenance: stiffness arrays: \nolinkurl{minimizeFlxPin.m} lines 387 and 400; \nolinkurl{minimizeExtX3.m} lines 575--584; \nolinkurl{minimizeExt.m} line 352; \nolinkurl{minimizeFlx.m} line 314. \texttt{fzero} force balance: \nolinkurl{minimizeFlxPin.m} line 438; \nolinkurl{minimizeExtX3.m} line 611; \nolinkurl{minimizeExt.m} line 389; \nolinkurl{minimizeFlx.m} line 352]. The per-configuration stiffness arrays and their series combinations are given in Table~\ref{tab:app_stiffness_arrays}.

