The audit and the prune are also why the laboratory built its own explorer app rather than adopting an existing tool. Airtable remains the working home of the data, but it is a curation tool, not a reader: it cannot travel past the laboratory's account, and a flat grid answers none of the questions this corpus exists to answer. An off-the-shelf bibliometric viewer was tried first and retired within weeks; it drew one crowded picture of the whole corpus, offered no drill-through from a cluster to the papers inside it, and let a handful of mega-cited classics dominate every view. The query patterns this resource must serve are specific: filter by afferent class, animal preparation, or pathway; follow a pathway record to the papers that established it in each species; walk the citation neighborhood of a candidate anchor paper; count the corpus by any two dimensions at once. A circuit designer wiring a reflex, an animal researcher choosing a preparation, and a roboticist choosing a sensor analog ask different questions of the same records, and the tools evaluated for this project did not provide the required combination of views. The corpus itself was therefore packaged as a laboratory tool: a single-file, offline explorer embedding the audited corpus, the citation graph, and the named clusters, with searchable and pivotable tables, focus-mode citation neighborhoods drawn in the laboratory's own convention of papers as neurons with excitatory and inhibitory synapses, and a generated knowledge base with an index page plus cluster, pathway, afferent, and animal pages, all distributed as a zip that runs in a browser on any machine with no network connection.

\section{Audited Corpus and Curation Coverage}\label{sec:sadb_corpus}

At the close of the curation effort the working base held 957 records: 561 papers, 39 feedback-pathway records, 244 model records, 109 review records, and 4 recorder entries, 43 records below the laboratory's 1,000-record operating cap, with room to grow. Coverage of the retained papers is concentrated where the laboratory's design and experimental work needs it. Of the 561 retained papers, 523 carry curation notes, 426 carry animal-preparation tags, 240 carry notes assessing each paper's animal-study potential, 118 are linked to named feedback pathways, 86 carry an afferent classification, and 258 carry notes translating the paper's findings toward robot or simulation use; 550 carry DOIs, and 38 remain bare pending a findable text.

The citation graph was built over the full audited corpus of 943 papers, which includes the 382 records the prune later removed and which the packaged database retains under an archived flag. Within that corpus, 730 papers are connected by 11,130 directed in-corpus citation pairs, and a Louvain community partition of the graph resolves 18 named topic clusters, from the classics of afferent locomotor control and spinal interneuron circuit modeling through human balance and posture to bioinspired flow sensing and deep-learning methods. The partition earned its keep in the prune: the satellite clusters it exposed, the flow-and-flight sensing, fish swimming, and neural-network method clusters, match the topic groups the first prune stage removed. Within that audited corpus, 833 papers carry curation notes and 318 carry attached PDFs, and the prune's 382 removed records remain in the package under an archived flag so the audited scope stays reconstructible in full. The corpus, its citation graph, its cluster assignments, and the flat corpus export are distributed together in the packaged explorer, and Figure~\ref{fig:sadb_explorer} in Appendix~\ref{app:sadb_records} shows its table view. Figure~\ref{fig:sadb_clusters} shows the same corpus as the packaged citation graph: the bubble map draws every paper as a neuron, its soma sized by citation count, and focusing a paper colors its direct citation neighborhood by direction.



