\subsection{Adapting Integrate-and-Fire Cells and Hybrid Synapses}

Between spikes, every spiking cell of the MuJoCo mirror obeys the membrane equation
\begin{equation}
    C_{m}\,\dot{V} \;=\; -\,g_{m}\left(V - V_{\text{rest}}\right)
        \;+\; I_{\text{syn}} \;+\; I_{\text{bias}},
    \label{eq:app_lif}
\end{equation}
with a spike emitted when $V$ reaches the firing threshold $\theta$, after which $V$ resets to $V_{\text{reset}}$ and $\theta$ increases by the increment $\Delta\theta$; between spikes $\theta$ decays back toward its initial value with time constant $\tau_{\theta}$, bounded by the adaptation floor. The tonic bias $I_{\text{bias}}$ parks the equilibrium a few millivolts below threshold, reproducing the analog cells' near-threshold operating point. Table~\ref{tab:app_spk_cells} lists the per-class values as coded (SNS-Toolbox's \texttt{SpikingNeuron} class; the toolbox's membrane update is dimensionally consistent only at $g_{m} = \qty{1}{\micro\siemens}$, which the mirror therefore uses throughout). The rhythm-generator adaptation time constant reuses the analog network's persistent-sodium inactivation value, so that tuned adaptation values transfer between builds; at the rhythm gate's operating point it is \qty{0.35}{\second}. The mechanism behind that value, recorded here rather than in the chapter body, is as follows. In the analog rhythm cells of the contact-driven cores, burst termination is carried by a persistent sodium current, a slowly inactivating inward current; in every port of this dissertation its inactivation time constant is held fixed rather than voltage-dependent, because the voltage-dependent form of the AnimatLab original quenches burst termination and leaves the network latched in a static state, the failure documented with the archived-model results of Chapter~\ref{ch:sns}.

