\section{Connectomes of the Spinal-Network Models}\label{app:connectomes}

The connectome of the production gait2392 spinal network (the 410-neuron SNS-Toolbox instantiation of Section~\ref{sec:fw_cpg}) is documented in Figures~\ref{fig:app_circuit_full} and~\ref{fig:app_circuit_core}. Both schematics are generated by \nolinkurl{Code/MuJoCo_SNS/spinal/draw_circuit.py} from the compiled network at the fitted walking configuration (\nolinkurl{fitted_walk_params.json}, the IK/NNLS back-solve refit, in which the pattern-formation and posture weights are refit against muscle activations back-solved from recorded walking kinematics, Section~\ref{sec:sns_tuning_methods}; 410 neurons, 201 per leg plus 8 shared, and 376 inputs; the eight shared cells are the single \texttt{DRIVE} and \texttt{POSTURE} descending neurons and the six balance cells \nolinkurl{BAL_PF}, \nolinkurl{BAL_DF}, \nolinkurl{BAL_TRK_EXT}, \nolinkurl{BAL_TRK_FLX}, \nolinkurl{BAL_LAT_R}, and \nolinkurl{BAL_LAT_L}), with every motoneuron-pool membership pulled live from \nolinkurl{muscle_map.py} rather than hardcoded, so the drawings document the compiled configuration used to generate them. The caption of Figure~\ref{fig:app_circuit_full} and its alt-text sidecar repeat these population counts, providing a cross-check on the stated configuration. Synapse markers follow the project convention throughout: open circles are neurons, white triangles mark excitatory synapses, and solid black dots mark inhibitory synapses.

Figure~\ref{fig:app_circuit_full} draws both legs in full. A vertical dashed midline separates the sides and a pale central corridor gathers the interleg commissural inhibition. Each side is organized in labeled layer bands: descending DRIVE (MLR surrogate), POSTURE, and balance inputs; the rhythm-generator half-centers with their mutual inhibition and adaptation cells; the phase-window pattern-formation cells; the Ia, II, and Ib afferent populations with the stance-gated Ib load-sharing interneurons; and the ten motoneuron pools per side (HIP-E 7, HIP-F 4, KNEE-E 4, KNEE-F 4, ANK-PF 9, ANK-DF 3, HIP-AB 6, HIP-AD 6, TRK-E 1, and TRK-F 2, that is 46 motoneurons per leg) driving their muscles. Figure~\ref{fig:app_circuit_core} shows the same network for one leg as a DRIVE/POSTURE to rhythm-generator to pattern-formation to motoneuron hierarchy, with the solved standing bias injected into every pool, the sensory column of Ia, II, and Ib afferents, and a boxed key listing every pool abbreviation with its member muscles. The spiking mirror of Section~\ref{app:spiking_details} replicates this topology exactly (Table~\ref{tab:app_spk_gates}, topology-mirror gate), so these two figures also serve as the connectivity reference for that model.

